  "query_preview": "..\n\nAggressive low and mid level drying outpaced last evening's 00Z \nmodels, yielding clear skies since the mid-morning hours. GOES water \nvapor imagery continues to show widespread subsaturated air \nencompassing the Great Lakes while the exiting low's cold front \nslowly clears toward the Michigan/Ohio border. Taking into \nconsideration the slow frontal progress, uninhibited insolation, and \n850 mb temperatures hovering near 17C over Metro Detroit, recording \nbreaking heat remains in reach today, given the previous record of \njust 83F. Further north, post-frontal flow has flipped west-\nnorthwesterly which will spread southeastward through the evening \nhours. The near-surface layer along the frontal slope cools faster \nthan the rest of lower column, leading to a more abrupt end to the \nbreezy afternoon conditions. Wind speeds drop off late tonight as \npressure gradient influence shifts from the low to an upstream ridge \nof high pressure. \n\nThe 595 dam 500 mb Gulf Coast ridge remains anchored tonight as a \nplume of 800-600 mb moisture releases anticyclonically from the \neastern Plains into the Ohio Valley amidst backing westerly flow. \nMeanwhile, the frontal boundary stalls just south of the state line \nas perturbed flow starts to work through aloft, helping trigger a \nperiod of scattered showers. Models preserve a relatively narrow QPF \nfootprint with the highest totals (up to 0.25 inches) along/south of \nI-94, mainly over the southern four counties. Light/trace amounts \nare possible further north, up to M-59. In spite of RRFS forecast \nsoundings showing an inversion layer from 2.5-6.5 kft, lapse rates \nabove should suffice in generating pockets of 100-250 J/kg of \nelevated CAPE. As a result, a few rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled \nout. \n\nThere could still be a few lingering showers exiting the area by \nsunrise Wednesday, followed by mainly dry conditions settling for \nthe rest of the daylight hours. Broad surface high pressure \ninfluence extending from Manitoba to Southeast Michigan ushers in a \ncooler continental airmass. The resulting shift to northerly flow \nhelps to nudge the existing vicinity boundary further into the Ohio \nValley throughout the day. Weaker winds expected as split-flow \nensues below 700 mb, limiting gusts to less than 15 mph. The next \nround of showers arrives Thursday as a southern stream closed low \nlifts from the desert southwest into the Plains, phasing with a \ndigging positively tilted longer wavelength trough. Supporting \ndynamics and a spike in upper level divergence will engage a robust \ntheta-e plume lifting up the Mid-Mississippi Valley and eventually \nbringing rain to southern Lower Michigan. High uncertainty exists in \nthe onset time for rain, reflected by the gradual northeastward \nexpansion of Chance PoPs after 12Z, per NBM. The bulk of the \nrainfall should occur Thursday evening, or early Thursday night as \nthe northeast flank passes over Southeast Michigan, well downstream \nof the deepening surface low, still located near the southern shores \nof Lake Michigan. The low then traverses southern Lower Michigan \nlate Thursday night before crossing into Lake Huron Friday morning.\n\nTurning colder and much windier on Friday as the system's cold front \nclears through. Aggressive constriction of the pressure gradient is \npossible in its wake, although there is a noticeable difference in \nthe magnitudes of the deterministic solutions. The column will cool \nat similar rates, but should see the mixed-layer expand sufficiently \nfor 35-45 knot lower tropospheric flow to mix down. Gusts in the 35-\n45 mph range cannot be ruled out Friday afternoon and evening. \nAdditionally, a deformation axis trails, which could help spur some \nlight scattered rain showers. Chilly overnight with temperatures \ndropping near or slightly below freezing, particularly north of I-\n69. Turning drier through the weekend with slightly warmer readings \nby Sunday, returning to climatological averages.",
